Integrating OpenVMS With Windows NT

Wes Melling, vice president, OpenVMS Systems Group

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Digital is teaming with Microsoft Corporation to provide an integrated
   systems environment that builds on the synergy between OpenVMS and
   Windows NT software

o  OpenVMS provides unlimited, enterprise high-end functionality for Windows
   NT and MS Windows based systems, as well as seamless integration of
   applications on OpenVMS, Windows NT, and Windows based systems

o  Develop applications on Windows NT and deploy them on Windows NT and
   OpenVMS

o  Thousands of applications written for OpenVMS will be augmented by
   applications now available or being developed for Windows NT

o  Three-tier client/server architecture expands benefits of client/server
   computing to address corporate level requirements

o  Reduce application development and maintenance costs

DESCRIPTION

Digital's OpenVMS systems provide industry-leading scalability,
availability, security, integrity, and interoperability for business
critical applications in a 24 hour per day, 365 days per year computing
environment. Digital is enhancing OpenVMS to ensure that it maintains this
market leading position.

Digital is teaming with Microsoft Corporation to develop an integrated
systems environment that creates synergy between the unequaled,
bet-your-business capabilities of OpenVMS and the state-of-the-art
functionality and growing applications library of Windows NT. This new
direction enables OpenVMS users and Digital business partners to capitalize
on the enterprise strengths of OpenVMS while dramatically increasing the
number of applications available to them.

OpenVMS and Windows NT software share a common heritage expressed through a
highly similar system design and distributed system capabilities. For this
reason, Digital believes that combining the best features of Windows NT with
the best features of OpenVMS will establish an environment that provides
OpenVMS and Windows NT users with the richest set of applications, tools,
and overall functionality.

Corporations can start today to take advantage of the business critical
capabilities of OpenVMS, and incorporate Windows applications into a
seamless three-tier client/server environment as they develop. All of the
applications, as well as all required data and information, can become
available to the desktop user without requiring that the user be aware of
where the applications or data reside.

OpenVMS strategy

Digital's OpenVMS strategy is to provide unlimited high end functionality to
the Windows environment and seamlessly integrate the business critical,
bet-your-business capabilities of OpenVMS systems with the Windows
environment.

Under this strategy, Digital and its software partners will provide the
tools and products necessary to ensure a seamless integration between
OpenVMS and Windows NT. As a result, application developers will be able to
develop their applications once on Windows NT and deploy them on OpenVMS
and/or Windows NT. This strategy will provide corporations and application
software providers with a tremendous savings in development and maintenance
costs. It will also make a very large library of applications available to
OpenVMS users.

In addition to providing OpenVMS users with an integrated systems
environment, Digital and its partners will also deliver all of the tools and
products necessary for unprecedented interoperability between OpenVMS and
Windows NT. All applications developed for Windows NT will be able to take
advantage of the benefits of OpenVMS for scalability, availability,
security, and integrity. Moreover, OpenVMS will provide interoperability
with UNIX systems through industry standards such as POSIX, XPG, DCE, and
CORBA.

Unlike IBM and HP, Digital recognizes the market reality that application
developers creating server applications are gravitating to both UNIX and
Windows NT. Thus, the company continues to enhance its leadership position
in both environments. In April, for example, Digital and Oracle Corporation
announced a major breakthrough in the UNIX industry, the world's first
64-bit database. Digital is also working to bring Windows to the enterprise
with value-added software, systems, and services. Now, with Microsoft,
Digital is integrating its OpenVMS strategy with Windows NT to provide
enterprise-strength client/server solutions.

Three-tier client/server computing

The full potential of the OpenVMS and Windows NT synergy is realized through
a three-tier client/server model of computing. The three software tiers are
characterized as follows:

o  The presentation tier, or desktop, which is dominated by personal
   computers.

o  The application or functional logic tier, which governs the rules by
   which business applications operate.

o  The third tier, where data, mission-critical applications, and services
   are managed.

Each software tier is architecturally separate within the overall
application. The three tiers are integrated using market-accepted middleware
such as ObjectBroker, COM/OLE, and DECmessageQ software.

Under this three-tier approach, users who have standardized on Windows at
the desktop can write presentation logic for the desktop, and can write
application logic in portable code for Windows NT and OpenVMS servers.
OpenVMS systems serve as the overall data repository for the Windows NT
systems and continue to house existing applications. As a result, the new
desktop and LAN based applications can access the same data as existing
OpenVMS applications, and business-critical enterprise data is kept secure
on the third tier.

With this three-tier approach, users can deploy applications across multiple
vendor platforms and operating systems while maintaining the flexibility to
move easily from one to another. They can also access the largest
application base in the world, Windows, within an environment that uses the
latest technology -- all while their business-critical enterprise
applications reside on reliable OpenVMS servers.

OpenVMS: The unlimited high-end for Windows

Digital is constantly enhancing the capabilities of OpenVMS to maintain its
market leading position. Some of the upcoming enhancements are:

o  A new log-structured file system that stores up to 10 TB of online data.
   This capability will enable OpenVMS to backup 1 TB of data in just eight
   hours, further enhancing the 24 x 365 availability of the OpenVMS
   environment.

o  Full 64-bit addressing, offering a practically unlimited address space
   for large applications plus much higher performance.

o  Memory channel interconnect, providing application interoperability at
   memory speeds instead of bus speeds.

o  Support for kernel threads, which will enable true parallel processing
   for increased performance of critical applications.

o  Enhanced OpenVMS Cluster capabilities to extend clusters over hundreds of
   miles. These enhancements will significantly improve the disaster
   tolerance capabilities of OpenVMS systems.

o  A full native implementation of TCP/IP, which will remove any
   dependencies on DECnet software to provide true multivendor,
   multiprotocol interconnect.

Seamless integration with Windows NT

There is already a lot of integration between OpenVMS and the Windows
environment, including:

o  Remote management via POLYCENTER NetView NT
o  DCE on OpenVMS and Windows NT
o  DECnet on Windows NT
o  OpenVMS cluster-wide PC-LAN support with PATHWORKS software (LAN Manager)
o  Reliable Transaction Router (RTR) on Windows NT and OpenVMS
o  OLE Portal with ObjectBroker on OpenVMS and Windows NT
o  DECmessageQ on OpenVMS and Windows NT
o  ObjectBroker on OpenVMS and Windows NT

Digital is also investing in many areas to seamlessly integrate OpenVMS with
Windows NT. Key capabilities to be added are:

o  A Windows NT client for the new OpenVMS log-structured file system. This
   capability will make all of the functionality and availability of this
   state-of-the-art file system transparently available to Windows NT
   applications.

o  WIN32 APIs, MFC libraries, and OLE2 to OpenVMS. These features will
   include WIN32s, WIN32, and the appropriate Microsoft Backoffice APIs.

o  Windows NT support to DECadmire software, so the software can generate
   applications to run on OpenVMS or Windows NT.

o  Clustering capabilities on Windows NT, bringing OpenVMS Cluster
   capabilities and benefits to Windows NT.

o  ALL-IN-1 client PC services on Windows NT, which will provide seamless
   integration of today's most popular office environment across the three
   tiers.

o  Microsoft dialects to Digital C++, to further support the seamless
   integration between OpenVMS and Windows NT.

o  An ObjectBroker tool on OpenVMS, to encapsulate traditional applications.

o  Common management objects for OpenVMS and Windows NT clusters, plus
   common high-level APIs and tools for load balancing across a cluster and
   application failover within a cluster.

Application development plans

Today, experienced application developers can develop applications once and
then deploy them on either OpenVMS or Windows NT. However, this capability
does require a fair degree of familiarity with OpenVMS, Windows NT, and
various tools to assist in the development and deployment.

Over time, as Digital and its partners complete their scheduled
deliverables, applications developers will be able to develop once on
Windows NT and not have to know on which platform the application will be
deployed.

Starting in July, Digital will offer training courses to help application
developers and architects begin writing for the new environment. It's
anticipated that they will immediately apply what they learn.

BENEFITS

o High availability -- OpenVMS servers and clusters provide Windows-based
  applications and file servers with industry-leading system availability
  for services and data access, 24 hours per day, 365 days per year.

o Data integrity -- Data integrity requirements are uniquely supported by
  proven data integrity features. The new log structured file system, which
  continuously backs up online data, further extends OpenVMS data integrity
  features.

o System and network management -- Digital's POLYCENTER NetView products
  will manage large networks of systems, including OpenVMS, UNIX, and
  Windows NT.

o High performance -- Industry-leading performance is provided through the
  Alpha 64-bit architecture and the new log structured file system.

o UNIX interoperability -- The OpenVMS and Windows NT environment
  interoperates with UNIX through a wide array of industry standards, such
  as POSIX, XPG, and CORBA.

o Flexibility -- Applications can be developed once, deployed across
  multiple platforms, and re-deployed as requirements change or a change in
  vendor is desired.

o Cost-effectiveness -- Low-cost, industry-standard PC tools can be used to
  develop applications for the OpenVMS and Windows NT environment.

o Business advantages -- OpenVMS users will have access to the fastest
  growing Windows NT server application portfolio, running on both Windows
  NT and OpenVMS.

