Positioning Digital's Firewall Products for Internet Security

HIGHLIGHTS:

o  The Digital Firewall Service is a high-end software and service offering
   that is delivered with custom-tailoring to the client's particular
   requirements

o  The Digital Firewall for UNIX offering is a mid-range pre-configured
   firewall that combines packet filtering, trusted application gateways,
   comprehensive logging, configuration, and a GUI

o  Digital is now also offering Border Network Technologies' BorderWare
   Firewall Server as its entry-level, low-cost product

DESCRIPTION

The Internet is growing at an exponential rate. Current commercial-only
domains will soon exceed 25,000, with that number doubling every year for
the foreseeable future. With this high rate of interconnectivity comes an
acute sense of the risk involved in connecting to the Internet and the
critical need for network security. Indeed, some analysts have identified
security as the key enabler to the continued growth of the Internet.

The companies connecting to the Internet represent a widely diverse group
with different needs; at most, 1,000 of these companies are part of the
Fortune 1000. Thus, a single network security product is unlikely to meet
the needs of this diverse group.

With its new firewall offerings, Digital is differentiating firewall
products into three segments: high-end, mid-range, and entry-level. These
offerings give Digital the most comprehensive firewall security products in
the industry, enabling it to accommodate the smallest to largest
enterprises, and the simplest to most complex needs.

High-end: the Digital Firewall Service

The Digital Firewall Service, a high-end offering combines software and
service (custom installation) and is delivered with custom-tailoring to the
user's particular security policy, configuration, and performance
requirements. Since it is a custom service, Digital can accomodate arbitrary
security policy needs and performance requirements. For example, data
streams flowing across the firewall can be rate-limited; Internet services
such as mail and news can run on dedicated servers for optimized
performance; and multiple routers can be used for non-stop Internet
connectivity. Such a system is usually a large, corporate Internet gateway
built as a multi-host firewall. Digital's Firewall Service software runs on
Digital UNIX based Alpha systems. The service includes the license for
Digital Firewall for UNIX (see below) as part of the base price, and allows
the user to build a firewall with one or multiple systems.

Mid-range: Digital Firewall for UNIX

The Digital Firewall for UNIX software is a pre-configured mid-range
firewall that combines packet filtering, trusted application gateways,
comprehensive logging, configuration, and a GUI. The product is installed on
a single Digital UNIX based Alpha workstation and can be expanded into a
multi-system firewall to meet future performance or availability
requirements as the organization's needs change. The mid-range product can
easily conform to adhoc security policy demands, and therefore scales
gracefully toward the high end.

Entry-level: the BorderWare Firewall Server

Digital is now offering Border Network Technologies Inc.'s BorderWare
Firewall Server V3.0 as its entry-level product. Entry-level, high-volume
users require a low-cost firewall that is secure, easy to install and
manage, and as close to plug-and-play as possible. The BorderWare product
includes a simple menu of security and configuration options from which a
site can choose. In addition to providing a complete suite of firewall proxy
services for security, the BorderWare product also provides a complete set
of basic Internet application servers, including WWW, anonymous FTP, mail,
and news (see "Border Network Technologies' BorderWare Firewall Server V3.0"
in this issue). It is both a firewall and Internet server, providing
plug-and-play Internet security and services in one product. Its tiered
usage pricing makes it ideal for small to medium size enterprises that want
solid Internet security and connectivity. The BorderWare product runs on
standard Intel PCs with Berkeley Software Design, Inc. (BSDI) UNIX software
(included), making the hardware platform among the least expensive
available. All of the software required comes on a single CD-ROM.

Positioning Digital's Firewall Offerings

The chart that follows shows the chief features that characterize high-end,
mid-range, and entry-level firewalls.

                           Entry-level    Mid-range          High-end

Security policy		   Default menu	  Default menu	     Customized
configurability		   with limited   with modifica-
			   flexibility	  tions allowed

Scalability		   Bounded	  Expandable	     Open-ended
			   load capacity

Redundancy		   None		  Optional	     Standard

Typical number of 	   <100, mostly   50-500 PCs, Macs,  >500 work-
systems on 		   PCs, Macs	  UNIX workstations  stations, PCs,
trusted network		   		  		     Macs

Topology of		   Simple, 	  LANs connected     Many LANs
trusted network		   single LAN	  by private WAN     connected by
			   		  		     private WAN
			   		  		     backbone

Packaged servers	   Yes, proxies	  N/A		     N/A
			   and servers

Services and 		   Minimum	  Optional 	     Required
consulting		   required,	  installation 	     consulting
			   optional	  and upgrade to     bundled with
			   installation   high-end firewall  software and
			   service 	  services	     installation
			   		  		     service

Security policy configurability, firewall scalability, and firewall
redundancy are the chief differentiators among entry-level, mid-range, and
high-end firewalls. Users with a particular security hot button may need to
have a security requirement implemented as a custom feature, and would
therefore require a custom, high-end firewall. For example, users who want
to rate-limit outbound FTP data streams for security reasons would need to
do so as a custom security policy on their firewalls, since data stream
rate-limits are not a standard feature in off-the-shelf firewalls. Users
satisfied with the security policy menu choices in an off-the-shelf firewall
would be good candidates for entry-level or mid-range firewalls. If these
users have a good chance of needing a custom security policy in the future,
a mid-range firewall would enable them to enjoy the benefits of an
off-the-shelf product today -- and to upgrade and customize the product in
the future, since Digital's mid-range firewall is scalable to the high end.

Depending upon the amount of traffic through a firewall, a user may need
dedicated servers to handle the news feeds, mail load, and other Internet
applications. A corporate firewall typically handles a great deal of
traffic. Digital's corporate firewall, for example, handles over two million
mail messages per month and provides a complete news feed to numerous sites.
Therefore, Digital's firewall is spread over many different systems with
dedicated servers -- a typical high-end firewall environment. Digital's
mid-range firewall is designed to be delivered on a single system, but
retains the ability to scale up to a multi-system firewall with dedicated
servers as requirements dictate. The mid-range solution is appropriate for
users who want a single-system solution today but whose growth demands may
dictate a multi-system solution in the future. The entry-level firewall is a
single-box, bounded system whose functions cannot be off-loaded to multiple
systems. What the entry-level firewall loses in scalability, it makes up for
in simple plug-and-play capability, making it an ideal high-volume solution
for users with straightforward security needs and lower throughput
requirements.

Users require different levels of Internet connectivity. A corporation or
enterprise of any size that conducts business on the Internet may require
non-stop connectivity, which requires a multi-host firewall with dual
routers configured with fail-over capability. Only a high-end firewall with
multiple hosts and routers can provide this redundancy. A mid-range system
that starts as a single host can be expanded to this configuration; an
entry-level firewall cannot.

