StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230 Options and Disk Drive Support

HIGHLIGHTS

o  New disk drive support on StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230

o  Cache memory for StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230 and cache memory
   battery backup for StorageWorks RAID Array 230

o  Upgrade program for StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230

o  StorageWorks RAID Array 230 subsystem supported on OpenVMS Alpha V6.2

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230 subsystems are hardware-based RAID
subsystems for Alpha and Intel system platforms that use the industry
standard EISA and PCI buses. They provide RAID levels 0 (striping), 1
(mirroring), 0+1 (striped mirrored sets), and 5 (striping with distributed
parity). By providing RAID functionality, they bind a collection of disk
drives into logical drives to provide data redundancy and increased
performance.

New disk drives are now supported on the StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230
subsystems. The following disk drives are now supported:

                              StorageWorks     StorageWorks
                              RAID Array 210   RAID Array 230

RZ26L-VW 16 bit, 1 GB	      N/A	       Yes
RZ28-VW 16 bit, 2 GB 	      N/A	       Yes
RZ29B-VA 8 bit, 4 GB	      Yes	       Yes
RZ29B-VW 16 bit, 4 GB	      N/A	       Yes

While it is not mandatory to upgrade the firmware for new disk drive
support, it is highly recommended that users adding new drives to their
existing RAID subsystems purchase the upgrade kit.

Cache Memory

Both the StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230 subsystems support optional
cache memory for increased subsystem performance. The subsystem controllers
are shipped with 4 MB of cache memory, which can be replaced by the 16 MB or
32 MB cache options now offered.

It is impossible to predict how cache will affect the performance of the
RAID subsystem without detailed knowledge of the application. However, tests
replicating a server environment with a mix of word processing and
spreadsheet applications on an OpenVMS Alpha application, using a DECpc AXP
150 with RZ26L disks in a RAID 5 configuration, indicated that:

o  Use of write-back cache usually improves performance dramatically.

o  Proper system tuning is vital in obtaining maximum performance.

o  Increasing cache size can provide large improvements in performance.

o  Effectiveness of the above factors will vary with application and
   workload.

Battery Backup

Implementing write-back cache memory can have a significant performance
impact. However, the use of write-back cache memory increases the risk of
data loss due to power failures. The battery backup option for the
StorageWorks RAID Array 230 reduces this risk. (Battery backup is not
available for the StorageWorks RAID Array 210.)

Write-through cache, which is the default mode for both subsystems, is a
cache implementation in which the system is notified that a disk write
operation has completed only after the data is on the disk. In a write-back
cache implementation, the system is notified that the write operation has
completed as soon as the controller receives the data, and before it is
written on the disk. While this process has the obvious advantage of
improving system performance, it also increases the risk of data loss. If a
power failure occurs after the controller has acknowledged receipt of the
data and before completion of the write operation to the disk, the data will
be lost without the system knowing it. This data loss occurs because the
cache memory is volatile and is erased if it is powered down. One way to
minimize this risk is to apply a battery to the cache memory module, so the
information will be retained in the event of a power failure.

Upgrade Program

The update includes the following changes to firmware, utilities, and
documentation:

o  New graphical user interface for OpenVMS Alpha for StorageWorks RAID
   Array 210 and 230

o  New serial terminal support (OpenVMS systems)

o  Support of four controllers per system

o  StorageWorks RAID Array 230 subsystem support in OpenVMS Alpha V6.2

The new online management utility for the OpenVMS graphical user interface
provides a Motif compliant window and menu driven environment for monitoring
and managing the RAID Array 200 subsystem. Functionality includes:

o  Viewing the status of the array
o  Check and repair parity of disk drives in the array
o  Rebuild a drive
o  Create hot spares
o  Set the rebuild rate
o  Troubleshooting

The new release of the StorageWorks RAID Array 230 subsystem will be
supported in OpenVMS Alpha V6.2.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The new options and upgrades for the StorageWorks RAID Array 210 and 230
subsystems are available now.

