RouteAbout Routers Connect Branch Offices Over The WAN

HIGHLIGHTS

*  Connects branch offices with Ethernet or Token Ring LANs
*  First members of a family of products that put routing where it is needed
   across the enterprise
*  Integrates IBM SNA and IP networks with award-winning DLSw software

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The new RouteAbout product family connects branch offices to the network as
part of Digital's enVISN network architecture for building virtual networks.
Digital's modular routing approach puts routing where it is needed  in the
RouteAbout Access routers for branch sites, the RouteAbout Central routers
for central sites, and in the DECswitch 900 products for building virtual
LANs.  All of these products use the same routing software and are modular
for use either standalone or within the DEChub family, providing unparalled
scalability as the network grows.

Branch offices with Ethernet or Token Ring LANs need to communicate with a
central site or the backbone network. With limited technical expertise at
the remote site, the communications device must be easy to install,
configure,and manage, preferably from a central site. Users with legacy IBM
SDLC and LLC2 LAN equipment need to consolidate their SNA and LAN
internetworks to save on equipment, wide area telecommunications, and
management costs.

The RouteAbout Access EW and RouteAbout Access TW routers connect remote
offices with Ethernet or Token Ring LANs respectively to a central site or
the backbone network. In addition to the LAN interface, each router has two
T1/E1 serial lines for connection to private lines or public network
services such as X.25 or Frame Relay. These routers can be used standalone
or in the DEChub network. The RouteAbout Access EW is a DEChub 90 module,
which can also be used in the DEChub 900 MultiSwitch. The RouteAbout Access
TW is a half-height DEChub 900 module.

Digital will ship the first RouteAbout Central router for central sites in
late 1995. This router will provide cost effective connectivity to multiple
remote branch sites over public and private network services. The RouteAbout
Central 900EW product will provide two Ethernet and eight T1/E1 interfaces
in a DEChub 900 compatible module. The RouteAbout family is designed to
interoperate with other RouteAbout routers, the DECswitch router, and DECNIS
routers over leased lines.

The features provided by the common routing software of the
RouteAbout and DECswitch 900 routers are:

*  Bridging -- Transparent (802.1d) Bridging, Source Route
   Bridging (SR), and Source Route Translational Bridging (SR-TB)
*  Routing -- TCP/IP, IPX, AppleTalk, DECnet Phase IV, and DECnet/OSI

Routing features include:

*  Protocols -- OSPF/MOSPF/RIP/EGP, Integrated ISIS, IPX RIP, and RTMP.
   WAN -- PPP, Frame Relay, X.25, Dial Backup, and Bandwidth Reservation.
   SNA -- Data Link Switching (DLSw) and SDLC Relay. The DLSw implementation
   is compatible with the IBM 6611 router.
*  Management -- Configuration and management via TCP/IP Telnet; monitoring
   via SNMP network management stations; and DEChub configuration via
   HUBwatch product.

The software is available in two packages:

IP Software      Multiprotocol Software

IP               All features of the IP package plus:
Bridging         IPX
PPP              AppleTalk
Frame relay      DECnet Phase IV
X.25             DECnet/OSI
Dial backup      DLSw
Bandwidth        SDLC Relay
reservation

BENEFITS

*  Modular routing -- The Bridge Router software, used on the RouteAbout
   Access, RouteAbout Central, and DECswitch products, puts routing where
   users need it across the entire network. Common software guarantees
   interoperability and reduces training and management costs.
*  Hub-based or Standalone -- Investment protection and incremental growth.
   Units can be used standalone and then placed in the DEChub environment,
   or the reverse, with no need to throw anything away.
*  DLSw -- Consolidates the SNA network onto the multiprotocol internetwork,
   eliminating the equipment,transmission, and management costs of running
   two separate networks. Locally terminates SDLC and LLC2 sessions to
   decrease timeouts and retransmissions and reduce WAN traffic. The DLSw
   software, acquired from Proteon, won the Data Communications Hot Product
   Award in 1994.
*  Adaptive source route bridging -- Routes some protocols while
   concurrently bridging others. Bridges nonrouteable and unsupported
   protocols. The RouteAbout Access TW supports both Transparent and Source
   Route bridging for use in mixed Ethernet and Token Ring environments.
*  Bandwidth reservation -- Guarantees classes of protocols access to the
   WAN bandwidth and ensures acceptable response times by prioritizing
   traffic within each class. Filters out traffic that should not traverse
   the WAN.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The RouteAbout Access EW will be available in July 1995 and the RouteAbout
Access TW will be available in August 1995 through Digital and its
authorized value-added resellers, distributors, and master resellers.
For initial purchase of a RouteAbout Access router, use the DE***-** order
